<urn:adk-prov:runs/data/229571cfdb8a4064b80847be7eff6f09> a lcont:LLMResponse ;
    dcterms:title "response:ProcessDescriptor_agent"^^xsd:string ;
    prov:value """```json
{
  "success": "Industrial Process Description: Synthesis of Octocrylene from Etocrylene and 2-Ethylhexanol\\n\\n### REACTION OVERVIEW\\nThis is an esterification reaction where the ethyl ester (Etocrylene) is transesterified with 2-ethylhexanol to produce the corresponding 2-ethylhexyl ester (Octocrylene), with ethanol as a byproduct.\\n\\n### PROCESS DETAILS\\n\\n**Reaction Type:** Transesterification/Alcoholysis\\n\\n**Energy Requirements:**\\n- Moderate heating typically required (60-120°C)\\n- Process is mildly exothermic\\n- Temperature control essential to prevent side reactions and preserve cyano group\\n\\n**Catalysts:**\\n- Typically acid catalysts: H2SO4, TsOH (p-toluenesulfonic acid), or Lewis acids\\n- Alternative: Tin(II) chloride (SnCl2) for transesterification\\n- Catalyst loading: 0.5-2% w/w typical range\\n\\n**Solvents:**\\n- Often solventless process (neat reactants)\\n- Alternatively: Toluene or xylene for better temperature control and equilibrium shift\\n- Solvent choice depends on desired reaction kinetics and recovery ease\\n\\n**Process Conditions:**\\n- Temperature: 80-110°C (optimal around 90-100°C)\\n- Pressure: Atmospheric or slight vacuum to remove ethanol byproduct\\n- Duration: 2-8 hours depending on temperature and catalyst\\n- Molar ratio: 2-ethylhexanol typically used in 1.2-1.5 fold excess\\n\\n**Equipment:**\\n- Jacketed reactor with stirring (mechanical or magnetic)\\n- Condenser for vapor recovery (especially ethanol)\\n- Optional: Dean-Stark trap to facilitate equilibrium shift by removing ethanol\\n- Vacuum pump for byproduct removal\\n\\n**Reaction Mechanism:**\\n1. Acid-catalyzed activation of carbonyl carbon\\n2. Nucleophilic attack by 2-ethylhexanol hydroxyl group\\n3. Tetrahedral intermediate formation\\n4. Proton transfer and ethoxy group elimination\\n5. Ethanol release and product formation\\n\\n### INDUSTRIAL CONSIDERATIONS\\n\\n**Yield Optimization:**\\n- Continuous ethanol removal drives equilibrium toward products (Le Chatelier)\\n- Excess 2-ethylhexanol (20-50%) ensures complete conversion of Etocrylene\\n- Reaction equilibrium typically favors products at process conditions\\n- Expected conversion: 95-99%\\n\\n**Product Recovery:**\\n- Distillation or precipitation-based separation\\n- Ethanol removal by vacuum distillation or azeotropic distillation\\n- Crystallization or column chromatography for final purification\\n- Octocrylene purity target: >98% for cosmetic/UV filter applications\\n\\n**Scale-up Considerations:**\\n- Exothermic reaction requires careful heat management at large scales\\n- Batch or semi-batch operation typical to maintain temperature control\\n- Stirring efficiency critical for homogeneous reaction\\n- Continuous process possible with plug flow or CSTR configuration\\n\\n**Special Considerations:**\\n- Cyano (C≡N) group is acid-sensitive under prolonged heating; careful catalyst selection and temperature control required\\n- Avoid bases that could degrade nitrile\\n- 2-Ethylhexanol highly viscous; adequate mixing essential\\n- Etocrylene (diphenyl compound) provides rigidity; ensure homogeneous mixing\\n- Product viscosity increases during reaction; stir power requirements increase over time\\n\\n**Typical Industrial Yield:** 90-95% (based on Etocrylene)\\n**Atom Economy:** 94.8% (minimal waste)\\n**Green Chemistry Aspects:** Solventless option available; ethanol byproduct recyclable; transesterification is inherently efficient\\n\\n**Applications:** Octocrylene is a UV absorber widely used in sunscreens and cosmetics (INCI approved)"
}
```"""^^xsd:string ;
