<urn:adk-prov:runs/data/9fd22bb978b1413d87a66caa450fc28b> a lcont:LLMResponse ;
    dcterms:title "response:ProcessDescriptor_agent"^^xsd:string ;
    prov:value """```json
{
  "success": "This is an ester hydrolysis process, specifically the saponification/hydrolysis of ethyl 2-(1-hydroxyethyl)hexanoate to produce 2-(1-hydroxyethyl)hexanoic acid and ethanol.\\n\\n**PROCESS DESCRIPTION:**\\n\\n**Reaction Type:** Ester Hydrolysis (Saponification)\\n\\n**Chemical Transformation:**\\nThe ethyl ester group (-COOC2H5) is cleaved by water, producing the free carboxylic acid (-COOH) and ethanol as byproduct. The hydroxyl group (-OH) on the adjacent carbon is preserved throughout the reaction.\\n\\n**Energy Requirements:**\\n- Typically requires moderate heating (60-80°C) to achieve reasonable reaction rates\\n- For industrial scale, temperatures of 70-90°C are common\\n- Reaction is slightly endothermic; minimal energy release expected\\n\\n**Catalysts:**\\n- Acid catalysis: H2SO4 (sulfuric acid) or HCl at 0.5-2 wt% concentration\\n- Alternatively: Aqueous acid medium without added catalyst (slower)\\n- Catalyst loading: typically 1-3 mol% relative to ester\\n\\n**Reaction Conditions:**\\n- Solvent: Water (primary solvent); may use aqueous ethanol mixture to improve miscibility\\n- pH: 1-3 (strongly acidic)\\n- Temperature: 70-85°C optimal\\n- Pressure: Atmospheric or slight positive pressure to minimize ethanol loss\\n- Reaction time: 2-6 hours depending on temperature and catalyst\\n\\n**Industrial Process Equipment:**\\n- Glass-lined or stainless steel reactor with mechanical stirring\\n- Temperature control system (heating jacket or internal coils)\\n- Condenser to recover ethanol vapors\\n- Separation equipment (decanter or centrifuge) for product isolation\\n- pH monitoring and control system\\n\\n**Process Details:**\\n- Molar ratio: Water in excess (typical 10:1 or higher) to drive equilibrium forward\\n- Stoichiometric ratio shown is 1:1, but industrial practice uses water excess\\n- Reaction follows typical esterification reverse kinetics (second-order)\\n- Equilibrium conversion typically 85-95% achievable\\n\\n**Separation & Recovery:**\\n- Ethanol (b.p. 78°C) can be recovered via distillation during reaction\\n- Product acid precipitates on cooling or acidification\\n- Extraction using organic solvent may be employed\\n- Acid-base extraction: convert to sodium salt, acidify to recover free acid\\n\\n**Special Considerations:**\\n- Chiral center at C-2 bearing hydroxyl group - stereoisomers may be present\\n- Alcohol functionality (-OH) makes product hydrophilic; affects separation strategy\\n- Ethanol evolution can be managed by controlled stripping\\n- Corrosion risk from acidic conditions; material of construction critical\\n- Environmental: acid catalyst recovery/neutralization required before disposal\\n\\n**Typical Yield:** 90-98% based on starting ester (theoretical maximum ~100%)\\n\\n**Scale-up Considerations:**\\n- Heat transfer management crucial for large-scale operations\\n- Ethanol vapor pressure (especially at >75°C) requires reflux condenser\\n- Acid catalyst corrosion becomes significant factor; stainless steel or glass-lined reactors preferred\\n- Safety: exothermic potential during rapid addition; staged feeding recommended"
}
```"""^^xsd:string ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:adk-prov:runs/prompt/ProcessDescriptor_agent_process_completion_d41d8cd98f00b204> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <urn:adk-prov:runs/process/f6467c7153af417591c0e77a82cb7775> .

